Now into the maps I have created some new fields-----------
**keys: the keys contain the pair [field, Name] => The field will be the data and the name will be the name of the data for display
these keys are what will appear in the toolbox as they do now. The display convention will be the same as it is now. 'See More' rule will remain the same.
The first field, name pair will be the header, and of course the name of it need not be shown as it is now.

**f_class_name: f_class_name is the features class name. Whenever in a map attributes there is this f_class_name it means the legends need to be shown as with current display with the RHD Road Network classification,
everything should follow the RHD Road classification display rules that are in place now.

**f_class_color: f_class_color is the color for the features. the features will be displayed with this color. 

**f_class_weight: This dictates the weight of the features in percentage. If the value is 0, then that feature will be hidden. 

Now this convention is done so that there is a general system which reads everything from the map data and nothing needs to be fixed afterward in the app js file.
Again, there will be the engineering palette set as before to be applied for those that does not have this fields.
In cases where there are no such fields then the common palette will be applied. 

There will be max 4 legends (no is defined by the number of classes in f_class_name) be shown at once below the layer name, just as it is shown currently with RHD Road Network layer.
When there are more than 4 such classes/legends 3 of them will be shown and there will be a circular button with the height of the texts. If that button is clicked a panel will appear at the right side with max height same as the height of the side panel at the left. Let's call it second panel. It will hover up over just as the side panel does now. Second panel will list all the classes and their respective display colors. The contents will be arranged from top to bottom and depending on the number of contents to accommodate—the second panel will be a scrollable one. This second panel will have a name at the bottom as header; the name being same as the layer name. In colors and behavior this second panel will be the same as that of the existing side panel. Only difference is that this second panel will have the pin button already activated when it comes off. Again, this second panel will not have any always visible part---because it only comes off when button from the side panel is clicked. Whenever this second panel appears the side panels pin will go deactivated and the panel will slide of to the side. However, when the second panel is already hovered and pinned if one can go for the side panel, bring it up and pin it too.

It's better to keep the legends in one line, and if it overflows then there will the space for the round button and
the contents will be cut from there to accommodate the button.
Again, as the 0 weight features are hidden, in the details of the second panel there will be (hidden) written after
the legend.

